C('Repeated bracket halving','width = 8.0\nfor _ in range(3):\n    width /= 2\nprint(width)','1.0',['4.0','2.0','0.5'],'Three halvings reduce eight to four, two and one.')
C('A bounded approximation loop','guess = 0\nsteps = 0\nwhile guess*guess < 30 and steps < 4:\n    guess += 1\n    steps += 1\nprint(guess, steps)','4 4',['6 6','5 4','4 5'],'The step limit is reached at four updates before the squared threshold is met.')
C('A bounded Newton update','x, target = 4.0, 9.0\nx = (x + target/x)/2\nprint(x)','3.125',['3.0','2.25','6.25'],'The specified square-root Newton step averages x and target divided by x.')
C('Bracketing a small positive square root','target = 0.25\nlow, high = 0.0, max(1.0, target)\nprint(low, high)','0.0 1.0',['0.0 0.25','0.25 1.0','1.0 1.0'],'For a target below one, the root can exceed the target itself; the unit upper bound encloses it.')
C('A cube-root guess check','target = -8\nfound = [g for g in range(-4, 1) if g**3 == target]\nprint(found)','[-2]',['[2]','[-8]','[]'],'The integer minus two cubes to minus eight.')
C('Grid points include both endpoints','step = 0.5\npoints = [i*step for i in range(5)]\nprint(points)','[0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0]',['[0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]','[0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0]','[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]'],'Five grid points at spacing one half cover zero through two inclusive.')
C('Keeping the best tested approximation','target = 7\ncandidates = [2.0, 2.5, 3.0]\nbest = min(candidates, key=lambda x: abs(x*x-target))\nprint(best)','2.5',['2.0','3.0','7'],'The squared residual magnitudes are three, 0.75 and two, so 2.5 is best among these candidates.')
C('A relative change calculation','old, new = 10.0, 9.5\nprint(abs(new-old)/abs(old))','0.05',['0.5','0.95','5.0'],'The absolute change one half is divided by the previous magnitude ten.')
C('A second-step bisection bracket','low, high = 0.0, 4.0\nfor _ in range(2):\n    mid = (low+high)/2\n    if mid*mid < 9:\n        low = mid\n    else:\n        high = mid\nprint(low, high)','2.0 3.0',['3.0 4.0','0.0 2.0','2.0 4.0'],'The first update raises low to two; the exact midpoint three then becomes high under the stated else rule.')
C('Counting until a squared threshold','n = 0\nwhile n*n < 18:\n    n += 1\nprint(n)','5',['4','6','18'],'Four squared is below eighteen; five squared is the first tested square at or above it.')
topic='strings'
T('For a nonempty string, why does text[-1] select the final character?', 'Negative sequence indices count backward from the end',['String indices start at one','Minus one always means an error','The first character has index minus one'],'Python normalizes negative indices relative to sequence length. An empty string has no final character and indexing it raises IndexError.')
T('Which method removes whitespace only at the edges?', 'strip()',['replace(" ", "") in every case','split()','find()'],'strip keeps interior whitespace while removing supported whitespace from both ends.')
T('Which expression checks whether text contains a substring key?', 'key in text',['text == key in every case','text is key','len(key) in text'],'Membership tests occurrence, which differs from requiring the whole strings to be equal.')
T('A string method such as lower() normally returns:', 'A new string value',['An in-place mutation of the original string','A numeric type code','A list of character indices'],'Strings are immutable; callers retain a transformed value by binding or using the returned string.')
T('Which indices does text[a:b] include when 0 <= a < b <= len(text) and the step is 1?', 'It starts at index a and stops before b',['Both endpoints in every case','Only index b','No indices below b'],'Under these bounds, the slice contains indices a through b-1 and has length b-a. The stop index b is excluded.')
topic='while'
T('What is the key step in exhaustive enumeration?', 'Generate candidates and test them against the stated condition',['Assume the first candidate succeeds','Discard untested candidates without a reason','Accept a plausible value without checking'],'Exhaustive search systematically checks the chosen candidate domain.')
T('For nonnegative square-root bisection, why compare mid squared with target?', 'The square function is increasing over nonnegative candidates',['Every midpoint is already the root','Negative values are always allowed without qualification','This comparison eliminates all rounding'],'The monotonic relation justifies deciding which half can contain the nonnegative root.')
T('Why include a maximum iteration count in an approximation procedure?', 'To provide a bounded failure path if the success criterion is not reached',['To guarantee an exact answer','To make every input take identical work','To replace the convergence criterion'],'An iteration cap limits work; it must be distinguished from evidence of convergence.')
T('A smaller grid step generally trades:', 'More candidate evaluations for finer resolution',['Less work for an exact answer always','No change in candidate count','Guaranteed physical validity'],'Covering a fixed interval at smaller spacing requires more candidate points but can resolve smaller differences.')
T('Which result should an approximate solver report when its cap is reached without meeting tolerance?', 'An explicit nonconvergence status under its contract',['An unqualified exact-solution claim','A fabricated zero','A guarantee that the model is wrong'],'The returned value and status must distinguish a stopped attempt from a converged result.')
